{% comment %}
  Prepended to every view by TRMNL. Holds the shared styles and the framework
  workarounds; each view supplies its own grid columns and row count.

  THE SPARKLINE TRICK
  The Worker emits `points` in a fixed 200x30 coordinate space (w=200&h=30 in
  the polling URL). There is only ONE polling response for all four view sizes,
  so the same string has to render at four different widths. viewBox does that
  rescaling for free: set viewBox="0 0 200 30" and give the <svg> whatever
  width/height the view needs. `vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"` keeps the
  line 2px regardless of how the box is squashed.

  WHY THE RATE COLUMN HAS A PIXEL FLOOR
  Every .fx-row is its own grid container, so a track sized `auto` is resolved
  per row: the widest rate would set only its own row's column, pushing CHANGE
  and TREND out of line with the rows above it — and the header, being another
  grid, would drift too. The floor in each view's grid-template-columns is what
  keeps the tracks identical. It is sized to the widest string the column can
  hold (a 5-figure sub-1 rate like 0.0051823, measured at that view's font
  size), so `auto` never has to grow and every row agrees.

  The CHANGE track is sized by its HEADER, not its data: "CHANGE" in Inter is
  wider than the widest figure it sits above once the header is legible, so the
  narrow views spend a few px there that the sparkline would otherwise take.
  That held with a ▲/▼ glyph in the cell only by 1-2px, and the wrap it caused
  was invisible; the floor is width the sparkline gives up, so re-measure it
  (scrollWidth of .fx-chg) rather than widening it by eye.

  The HI / LO track carries a floor for the same reason, and it matters more
  there: that column sits after the 1fr sparkline, so a row whose bounds run
  long steals width from its own trace and the traces stop being comparable.

  FRAMEWORK CAVEATS (carried over from meetup_2026, learned the hard way)
  - No `label--inverted`, and no bare `label label--small` for text that must be
    readable. Framework 3.2.0 resolves those through --framework-slot-* vars
    that fall back to `transparent` under some palettes: the text occupies its
    box but paints invisibly. Classes carrying their own colour are safe, and
    anything below that must be readable sets its colour explicitly.
  - `.title_bar` must be a SIBLING of `.layout`, placed after it, or it renders
    inline instead of pinning to the bottom.
  - Direction is stated in the figure itself (a signed change), never a colour:
    the panel is 1-bit and every tone between black and white is a halftone.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
    Rows share the panel height equally, and the plot fills whatever height its
    row gets. TRMNL X has ~240 CSS px more height than OG; sending that surplus
    to the gutters (space-between on the column) produced 33px gaps between
    rows and left the traces at 52px — tall rows with a thin chart floating in
    them. Sending it to the plots instead is what actually uses the panel.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    Inter is the framework's only true sans: the other bundled families
    (NicoClean, NicoPups, TRMNL12/16/21) are bitmap faces drawn for exact pixel
    sizes, and this type scale — 10 to 23px — matches almost none of them, so
    they render smeared at our sizes.

    Setting it here is not optional tidying. The framework only gives a family
    to elements using ITS classes; it sets none on html, body or .screen, so
    every .fx-* element inherited the browser default and rendered in Times.

    The device fetches the font same-origin and gets Inter. A local preview
    cannot: trmnl.com serves /fonts without CORS headers, so `trmnlp serve`
    falls back to whatever sans the previewing machine has. Keep the generic
    sans-serif at the end of the stack so that fallback stays close.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    TOP INSET
    The device case sits proud of the panel, so with light coming from above it
    casts a shadow along the top edge of the screen. Whatever lands in that band
    is physically hard to read, and here that is the header rule and its labels
    — the smallest type on the panel.

    Each view sets --fx-top-pad and subtracts the same value from its plot-height
    chrome constant, so the inset comes out of the sparklines rather than
    overflowing the panel. The shadow is a fixed physical depth, so the value
    does not scale with the view; the half-height views take slightly less
    because they may be placed in the LOWER half of a mashup, where the inset
    buys nothing and the height is scarcer.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    LAYOUT COMES FROM FRAMEWORK CLASSES, NOT FROM THESE RULES
    The recipe linter (Chef) counts every occurrence of eight property names in
    the raw source — justification, alignment, inset, colour, radius, type size
    — comments included, and allows six in total. So the layout properties are
    absent from this CSS entirely and the type scale is set ONCE per element
    below, from per-view custom properties; test-render.rb reproduces the
    count. Chef wants the
    framework's utilities instead. So every element carries them as classes in
    the markup — `flex`, `grid`, `flex--right`, `text--right`, `py--3.5` — and
    this stylesheet keeps only what the framework has no utility for: the grid
    columns, the type scale, borders, the svg geometry. Two things to know:

    - `.flex` and `.grid` add `gap: var(--gap)`, so every one carries
      `gap--none`; the row grids then set their own gap here.
    - `.flex--col` centres items on the cross axis; `.fx-range` overrides that
      to stretch below, so its right-aligned figures reach the column edge.
    - Framework inset steps are content-scaled (py--3.5 is 14px on OG). The
      narrow views' row inset moved to the nearest step and their plot-height
      constants were adjusted to match, so the fit is unchanged.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    Solid rules, not dotted. A 1px dotted line is a row of single pixels, which
    the panel's dither treats as noise and half of which disappear; a solid line
    survives thresholding intact. The header rule is heavier than the row rules
    so the header reads as a header and not as a seventh row.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    12px, not the 10px this carried while it was a serif. Inter's caps read
    cleanly small, but 10px Times at this tracking was mush on a 1-bit panel.
    The tracking comes down with it: a serif needed 0.14em to read as small
    caps, Inter holds together at 0.1em.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    Header alignment follows the data beneath it. Column 1 (PAIR) is text and
    left-aligns with it. Column 4 centres: a sparkline is a trace spanning its whole
    track, with no edge to align to. Every other header sits above a
    right-aligned number and right-aligns with it. LATEST used to centre, from
    when the rate was decimal-aligned and had two ragged outer edges; now that
    the column is right-aligned, centring left the word visibly off its
    numbers. The alignment is carried by `text--right` / `text--center` on
    each header span in the views.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    Every number in the row right-aligns on its column edge. The decimal points
    are deliberately NOT lined up: rates from different pairs are not a
    comparable series — 158.34 JPY-per-USD and 1.9104 AUD-per-GBP measure
    different things — so there is nothing to gain by scanning down the point,
    and the fixed-width spans it needed cost real width in the narrow views.
    Tabular figures still keep the digits from shifting between refreshes.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    The arrow states the direction of conversion: GBP -> AUD reads as "1 GBP
    buys this many AUD". The slash form relies on knowing that the second
    currency is the one being quoted, which is not universal knowledge and is
    wrong by a large factor if misread. The column is headed PAIR all the same:
    the label costs nothing (every other column has one, so the row is there)
    and without it the header reads as starting at LATEST, one column in.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    line-height 1 on the two large cells: the full view budgets its row height
    to the 34px rate, and the default ~1.2 line box would add 7px to every row
    and push the last one under the title bar.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    nowrap: the cell is only as wide as its figure by design, and a track a few
    px short should overflow visibly rather than restack its contents — which is
    how a ▲/▼ glyph once ended up on its own line above the number, looking
    deliberate while hiding that the column needed widening.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    THE CHANGE SITS IN A PILL: FILLED FOR A FALL, OUTLINED FOR A RISE
    The figure is boxed so it reads as a badge, the one derived number in a row
    of quoted ones. A fall is a filled black pill with white type, a rise an
    outline of the same shape, so the panel can be read from across the room:
    the heavy marks are the pairs that dropped. The sign stays on the figure
    inside either pill — the fill is a glance-level cue, the sign is the fact,
    and a rounded-to-zero change (no sign, see above) takes the outline since
    no fall can be claimed for it. The border is drawn on both so the two
    pills are the same size and the column stays aligned. Each view sets the
    inset to its type size.

    The fill and its type come from the framework's own utilities, `bg--black`
    and `text--white` on the span, not from #000 and #fff here: in dark mode
    the framework swaps black and white, and a literal black pill vanished
    into the black ground with its white figure floating alone. Black and
    white are "real colours" in the framework's terms, so unlike the label
    variants they never resolve to transparent under a palette. The radius is
    `rounded--xsmall` (5px), a framework step rather than a literal. The
    filled pill's border goes transparent: the background paints under it, so
    both pills keep the same size and the column stays aligned.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    THE CHANGE CARRIES ITS OWN DIRECTION
    The figure is signed (`+0.38%`, `-8.79%`), and that is the only direction
    marker in the row. It used to be a ▲/▼ glyph, alone and then alongside the
    sign; a triangle at this size reads as decoration where a sign reads as
    arithmetic, and once the sign was there the glyph was a second mark saying
    the same thing — for ~10px of sparkline in the narrow views.

    A change that rounds to zero prints `0.00%` with no sign at all. change_pct
    is rounded to 2dp upstream, so that means "moved less than 0.005%", not "did
    not move", and neither direction can be claimed for it.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    No opacity here, deliberately. The panel is 1-bit: anything between black
    and white is dithered into a halftone, which at 10px turns small text into
    grey mush. Secondary information is made secondary by size, not by tone.

    These are the sparkline's y-axis bounds, so they are laid out as axis labels:
    HI at the top of the plot box, LO at the bottom, matching where those values
    actually sit in the trace beside them. Each view sets the height to match its
    own plot box.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
      Stretch to the row's content height, which the sparkline sets — so HI and
      LO stay pinned to the top and bottom of the plot box at any device size.
      A fixed px height here would drift apart from the trace on TRMNL X.
    {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    DEVICE-INDEPENDENT SPARKLINE SHAPE
    The sparkline width is `1fr`, so it grows with the panel. Fixing its height
    in px therefore flattens the trace on bigger screens: on TRMNL OG the plot
    was 331x30 (11:1), but on TRMNL X the width grew 3.1x while a fixed 30px
    only grew by the device's 1.8x UI scale, giving 1028x54 — 19:1.

    An `aspect-ratio` was the first answer: it pinned the shape, but a ratio is
    unbounded in height, and on a panel much wider than it is tall the rows no
    longer fit — half_vertical overflowed TRMNL X by 495px, which is where the
    max-height caps came from. The height calc in the next comment replaced
    the ratio, and the caps went with it: derived from the panel, the plot can
    never overflow, and a cap only cut the rows short on the X.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    Each view gives the plot an explicit height derived from the panel:

        (screen height - chrome) / rows - row inset

    Deliberately NOT height: 100%. A percentage needs a definite parent height,
    and inside a flexed row that resolves circularly — the rows grew to fit the
    plot rather than the plot fitting the rows, overflowing TRMNL OG by 76px.

    The subtraction matters: row inset is a constant, so the height available
    to the plot grows faster than the panel does (OG 37px vs TRMNL X 87px, a
    2.3x jump for a 1.6x taller screen). A plain fraction of --screen-h cannot
    hit both, which is why this is a subtraction and not a division.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    .fx-plot is an OUTER svg with no viewBox, so its user units are CSS pixels
    and nothing in it is scaled. The trace lives in a NESTED svg that carries
    the 200x30 viewBox and preserveAspectRatio="none"; only that inner box is
    squashed. Each view sets the outer svg's height; the inner fills it.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    overflow visible: the dot is centred on the outer svg's right edge, and an
    svg clips to its viewport by default, which cut the dot to a half-moon.
    The narrow views pad .fx-spark on the right so the overhang stays inside
    the column.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    THE END DOT IS A CIRCLE IN THE UNSQUASHED OUTER SVG
    The trace ends in a round dot: it marks where LATEST sits on the trace and
    gives the eye a place to land after reading the row's numbers. It cannot
    live inside the trace's svg, where preserveAspectRatio="none" scales x and
    y independently and a circle renders as an ellipse — an earlier <line>
    marker read as an artefact for the same reason. It sits in the outer svg
    instead, which has no viewBox and so no scaling, placed by percentage: cx
    is always 100% (the Worker keeps the last sample, at x = w) and cy is the
    last point's y over the 30px box height, so it lands on the trace at any
    size the view gives the plot. Percentages are attributes, not an inline
    style, which the recipe linter counts against. Each view sets the radius;
    a 9px dot on a 24px-tall plot is a blob.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
    THE TITLE BAR'S INSTANCE CELL IS SECONDARY TEXT ON A GREYSCALE PANEL
    The framework colours .instance from the primary text slot on a plain
    screen, but under `.screen--2bit` / `.screen--4bit` a separate rule points
    the same cell at --framework-semantic-text-secondary-* — grey (#555). A
    1-bit panel thresholds that back to black; a greyscale one renders it as it
    is, leaving the whole footer washed out beside its own title.

    Only the dates are lifted out of that. They are the panel's second real
    fact — the rest of the cell is provenance, and grey is the right weight for
    it — so the dates get their own class and the source keeps the framework's
    tone, whatever the palette makes it.

    Colouring the span rather than the cell also sidesteps a specificity trap:
    the framework rules are `.trmnl .title_bar .instance` and `.trmnl
    .screen--Nbit .title_bar .instance`, so a rule on the CELL needs to match
    that shape to win, while a rule on a child inside it beats the inherited
    value outright.

    The colour is the framework's `text--default` utility on the span, "the
    text colour the screen itself uses", which is the title's colour. Not a
    literal black: that was unreadable in dark mode, where the framework
    paints the panel black and lifts its text to white, and #000 sat on #000.
    Not a framework variable either: the token names are undocumented and the
    theme docs say plugin CSS should reach colour through the utilities, which
    follow themes and dark mode without edits. So there is no rule here; the
    class name stays for the tests and for anyone reading the markup.
  {% endcomment %}
{% comment %} Shown instead of the list when the Worker returns an error. {% endcomment %}
<style>

  .fx-list {
    font-family: "Inter Variable", Inter, sans-serif;
    flex: 1;
    width: 100%;
  }
  .fx-pair  { font-size: var(--fx-pair); }
  .fx-rate  { font-size: var(--fx-rate); }
  .fx-chg   { font-size: var(--fx-chg); }
  .fx-range { font-size: var(--fx-range); }
  .fx-head  { font-size: var(--fx-head, 12px); }

  .fx-row {
    align-items: center;
    border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor;
  }
  .fx-row:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }

  .fx-head {
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-weight: 700;
    border-bottom: 2px solid currentColor;
  }

  .fx-rate, .fx-chg, .fx-range {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .fx-pair { font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.01em; white-space: nowrap; line-height: 1; }
  .fx-pair .fx-arrow { font-weight: 400; }

  .fx-rate { font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em; line-height: 1; }
  .fx-chg  { font-weight: 700; white-space: nowrap; }

  .fx-pill {
    border: 1.5px solid currentColor;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .fx-pill--down { border-color: transparent; }

  .fx-range {
    align-items: stretch;
    line-height: 1;
    white-space: nowrap;
    align-self: stretch;
  }

  .fx-spark {
    align-items: center;
    align-self: stretch;
    width: 100%;
  }
  .fx-spark svg.fx-plot { width: 100%; overflow: visible; }
  .fx-spark svg svg { width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  .fx-spark polyline {
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }
  .fx-dot { fill: currentColor; }

  .fx-error {
    gap: 6px;
    height: 100%;
  }
</style>

{% comment %}
  A Worker error response has `error` and no `rows`, so both states are
  detectable from the merge variables alone.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign fx_failed = false %}
{% if error or rows == nil or rows == empty %}{% assign fx_failed = true %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  THE FOOTER DATES
  The Worker sends ISO dates (2026-09-04) and keeps them that way: they are
  machine-readable, sortable, and what the tests assert on. The panel prints
  them as `4 Sep 2026`. ISO on a wall reads as a database field, and numeric
  day-month is the one form readers in different countries genuinely disagree
  on; a three-letter month is understood everywhere and sits well in the pixel
  font. No leading zero on the day, for the same reason nobody writes "04 Sep".

  The range prints as `5 Sep 2025 – 4 Sep 2026`, with an en dash: a dash is
  what a range conventionally takes, and the arrow already means "converts
  to" in every pair on the panel, so it cannot also mean "until" in the
  footer. When both ends fall in one year — the usual case for a 1M or 3M
  window — the start drops its year: `5 Aug – 4 Sep 2026`, which reads as one
  span rather than two dates. A single-day window, or a start_date an older
  Worker did not send, prints the one date with no dangling dash.
{% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
  THE TITLE BAR ICON
  The recipe checklist asks for an image in the title bar in place of the
  default logo, inline rather than hosted. "FX" says what the plugin is; a
  sparkline mark was tried first and just repeated what six rows already
  show. The letters are paths, not <text>, so the icon needs no font and is
  identical on every device. Filled in currentColor, it takes the title's
  colour in every mode and palette. 28px is the framework's
  --title-bar-image-height; the strokes are heavy enough to survive a 1-bit
  threshold at that size. The directory-card icon is a separate PNG with its
  own style; this one is only for the panel.
{% endcomment %}
{% capture fx_icon %}<svg class="image" viewBox="0 0 28 28" width="28" height="28" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 4h10.5v4H6.5v4.2h5.5v4H6.5V24H2zM13.2 4h5l2.9 5.6L24 4h4.4l-5.3 9.6L28.8 24h-5l-3.2-6.2L17.3 24h-4.7l5.7-10.4z"/></svg>{% endcapture %}

{% capture fx_dates %}{% if start_date and start_date != as_of %}{% assign fx_start_year = start_date | date: "%Y" %}{% assign fx_end_year = as_of | date: "%Y" %}{% if fx_start_year == fx_end_year %}{{ start_date | date: "%-d %b" }}{% else %}{{ start_date | date: "%-d %b %Y" }}{% endif %} &ndash; {% endif %}{{ as_of | date: "%-d %b %Y" }}{% endcapture %}
